Summary: Exit a process when its wineserver is gone
Category: system
Kind: fix
Origin: wine-mac-builds
Upstream: no
Why: A Wine thread only finds out that the wineserver has gone when its
 next request fails (abort_thread), and the process ends with its last
 Wine thread. A thread that only sleeps (a non-alertable Sleep is local),
 renders, or waits in a macOS run loop makes no request, so after the
 server died (killed, or crashed) such a process ran on with its windows
 and open files, and an app embedding Wine couldn't tell its program had
 gone. Now each process watches its socket to the server (fd_socket) with
 a kqueue in a thread of its own, with every signal blocked, and exits
 (status 1, "wine: the server is gone; exiting") when the server's end
 closes. The server closes that socket only when it exits or has killed
 the process, so nothing that should go on running is stopped.
Programs: Second Chance (an app that embeds Wine): games went on running
 after their wineserver was killed
Uncertain: macOS only (kqueue); other systems keep the old behaviour.
Evidence: On 11.22 (host10 tree, macOS 26.7.2), a program whose two
 threads only sleep was still running after wineserver -k9 (1 run, and
 the regression test after 10 s). With this change it exited at once in 3
 of 3 runs. In that same unfixed run, the prefix's explorer.exe (its
 desktop thread waits in a message loop) was still running half an hour
 after its wineserver was killed, and 12 service processes of two such
 test prefixes (services.exe, plugplay, svchost, winedevice) were still
 running nearly two hours later. On Second Chance's slot-1, a steam.exe
 whose wineserver had gone spun at 96% CPU for about six hours with its
 services and explorer, slowing the other tests on that Mac.
Status: 2026-11-22 (night): in a local test build (second local test build, macfixes-10.0-build0 of a243d7e, not
 published): server-gone
 passes (the program exited after its wineserver was killed; 1 run).
 The build's full suite: 51 passed, 0 failed, 0 skipped.
 2026-11-22 (evening): this revision writes the message with
 write() instead of MESSAGE(): in a local test build of the first revision
 (macfixes-10.0-build0, not published) server-gone failed 4 of 4, and the
 watcher thread was found stuck in wine_dbg_printf (10.0's debug output
 needs a TEB, which this thread doesn't have; 11.x copes). With this
 revision's ntdll.so swapped into that build, server-gone passes 3 of 3.
 Not yet in a build as it is now.
 2026-11-22: ported from 11.22 (applies unchanged); not yet in a build.
 server-gone fails on macfixes-10.0-build7 (1 run), as on 11.22
 before this patch.

diff --git a/dlls/ntdll/unix/server.c b/dlls/ntdll/unix/server.c
--- a/dlls/ntdll/unix/server.c
+++ b/dlls/ntdll/unix/server.c
@@ -66,6 +66,7 @@
 #ifdef __APPLE__
 #include <crt_externs.h>
 #include <spawn.h>
+#include <sys/event.h>
 #ifndef _POSIX_SPAWN_DISABLE_ASLR
 #define _POSIX_SPAWN_DISABLE_ASLR 0x0100
 #endif
@@ -1636,7 +1637,67 @@
 }
 
 
+#ifdef __APPLE__
 /***********************************************************************
+ *           server_watch_thread
+ *
+ * A thread only notices that the server is gone on its next request, and a
+ * thread that only sleeps, renders or waits in a macOS run loop makes none,
+ * so the process could outlive its server forever. Wait for the server's end
+ * of the socket to close, and exit then.
+ */
+static void *server_watch_thread( void *arg )
+{
+    int kq = (int)(INT_PTR)arg;
+    struct kevent ev;
+
+    for (;;)
+    {
+        int ret = kevent( kq, NULL, 0, &ev, 1, NULL );
+        if (ret == -1 && errno == EINTR) continue;
+        if (ret == -1) return NULL;
+        if (ret == 1 && (ev.flags & EV_EOF))
+        {
+            static const char msg[] = "wine: the server is gone; exiting\n";
+            /* not MESSAGE: this thread has no TEB for Wine's debug output */
+            write( 2, msg, sizeof(msg) - 1 );
+            abort_process( 1 );
+        }
+    }
+}
+
+static void start_server_watch( void )
+{
+    struct kevent ev;
+    pthread_attr_t attr;
+    pthread_t thread;
+    sigset_t all, old;
+    int kq;
+
+    if ((kq = kqueue()) == -1) return;
+    fcntl( kq, F_SETFD, FD_CLOEXEC );
+    /* EV_CLEAR: wake once per change, not for as long as fds wait to be received */
+    EV_SET( &ev, fd_socket, EVFILT_READ, EV_ADD | EV_CLEAR, 0, 0, NULL );
+    if (kevent( kq, &ev, 1, NULL, 0, NULL ) == -1)
+    {
+        close( kq );
+        return;
+    }
+
+    /* no Wine signal may be delivered to this thread */
+    sigfillset( &all );
+    pthread_sigmask( SIG_SETMASK, &all, &old );
+    pthread_attr_init( &attr );
+    pthread_attr_setstacksize( &attr, 64 * 1024 );
+    pthread_attr_setdetachstate( &attr, PTHREAD_CREATE_DETACHED );
+    if (pthread_create( &thread, &attr, server_watch_thread, (void *)(INT_PTR)kq )) close( kq );
+    pthread_attr_destroy( &attr );
+    pthread_sigmask( SIG_SETMASK, &old, NULL );
+}
+#endif
+
+
+/***********************************************************************
  *           server_init_process
  *
  * Start the server and create the initial socket pair.
@@ -1714,6 +1773,10 @@
     reply_pipe = init_thread_pipe( data );
     close( reply_pipe );
 
+#ifdef __APPLE__
+    start_server_watch();
+#endif
+
     SERVER_START_REQ( init_first_thread )
     {
         req->unix_pid    = getpid();
